   "text": "This is my reading of the drawing, written as a SMILES. It is a bicyclic amatoxin-type peptide (it looks like \u03b1-amanitin). I traced the ring from the Ile residue around the macrocycle and assigned each stereocentre from the wedges and hashes.\n\n**Residues and connectivity**\n- Ile has a sec-butyl side chain, with its NH acylated by a Gly.\n- That Gly is followed by Cys.\n- The Cys CH2 carries an S(=O) that bonds to C2 of the indole.\n- The indole is a 6-hydroxy-tryptophan, and its C3 carries the CH2 of the Trp side chain.\n- Trp is followed by the dihydroxy-isoleucine, which has CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH2OH on the alpha carbon.\n- Next come 4-hydroxyproline and then Asn, which carries a CH2C(=O)NH2 side chain.\n- The Asn NH closes the ring onto the Cys carbonyl.\n- A second Gly links the Trp carbonyl back to the Ile carbonyl.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nI derived the @/@@ marks for each centre from the drawn wedges and hashes and checked them against the L-amino-acid configurations.\n\nThe sulfoxide sulfur is the least certain centre. The drawing is ambiguous there, so I assigned the R configuration known for \u03b1-amanitin.\n\nSMILES: CC[C@H](C)[C@H]1C(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@H]2C[S@@](=O)c3[nH]c4cc(O)ccc4c3C[C@H](NC(=O)[C@H]([C@@H](C)[C@@H](O)CO)NC(=O)[C@@H]5C[C@@H](O)CN5C(=O)[C@H](CC(N)=O)NC2=O)C(=O)NCC(=O)N1"
